-- title:
--   Lemma 3.10 — the conditional descent inequality for MA-AdaOGD
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal G$ be a $\beta$-strongly monotone continuous game ($\beta>0$), let $G,D>0$ be such that the noisy gradients satisfy $\mathbb E[\xi^t\mid x^t]=v(x^t)$ and $\mathbb E[\|\xi^t\|^2\mid x^t]\le G^2$ for $t\ge1$, and $\|x-x'\|\le D$ for all $x,x'\in\mathcal X$. Let the iterates $\{x^t\}$ be generated by Algorithm 3 with horizon $T\ge1$, and let $x^\star$ be a Nash equilibrium (there is at most one). Write $\mathcal H=\sigma(\{\eta_i^t\}_{1\le i\le N,1\le t\le T})$. Then each $\|x_i^T-x_i^\star\|^2$ is integrable and, almost surely,
--   $$
--   \sum_{i=1}^N\eta_i^T\,\mathbb E\big[\|x_i^T-x_i^\star\|^2\,\big|\,\mathcal H\big]
--   \ \le\ \sum_{i=1}^N\eta_i^1\|x_i^1-x_i^\star\|^2
--   +D^2\sum_{t=1}^{T-1}\max\Big\{0,\max_{1\le i\le N}\{\eta_i^{t+1}-\eta_i^t\}-2\beta\Big\}
--   +G^2\sum_{t=1}^{T-1}\max_{1\le i\le N}\Big\{\frac1{\eta_i^{t+1}}\Big\}.
--   $$
--
--   This is the one-step descent inequality summed over rounds; the proof of Theorem 3.7 starts from it.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: $\mathbb E[\xi^t\mid x^t]=v(x^t)$, $\mathbb E[\|\xi^t\|^2\mid x^t]\le G^2$ (p.13) → conditioning on $x^t$ and all step sizes, $\mathcal H_t=\sigma(x^t)\vee\sigma(\{\eta_i^s\}_{1\le i\le N,1\le s\le T})$: $\mathbb E[\xi^t\mid\mathcal H_t]=v(x^t)$, $\mathbb E[\|\xi^t\|^2\mid\mathcal H_t]\le G^2$ a.s., with $\xi^t$ strongly measurable and $\xi^t$, $\|\xi^t\|^2$ integrable, for $1\le t\le T$; reason: the paper's proof (Appendix D, p.40) states exactly this conditioning, "$\{\eta_i^t\}$ are generated independently of any noisy gradient feedback, we have $\mathbb E[(x^\star-x^t)^\top\xi^t\mid x^t,\{\eta_i^t\}]=\mathbb E[(x^\star-x^t)^\top v(x^t)\mid\{\eta_i^t\}]$, $\mathbb E[\|\xi^t\|^2\mid x^t,\{\eta_i^t\}]\le G^2$"; conditioning on $\sigma(x^t)$ alone does not fix the joint law of the noise and the step sizes; the noise may depend on the past beyond $x^t$
--   - encoding: $\eta_i^1$ (used in Lemma 3.10, never defined by Algorithm 3) → $\eta_i^1=1$, the formula of line 6 with the empty maximum $0$; reason: the paper's proof holds for any $\eta_i^1$ and the proof of Theorem 3.7 uses $\eta_i^1\le1$
--   - encoding: $x^\star$, the unique Nash equilibrium → every Nash equilibrium $x^\star$; reason: the page's $x^\star$ is a Nash equilibrium; strong monotonicity allows at most one, so the statements coincide
--   - encoding: $\mathbb E[\|x_i^T-x_i^\star\|^2\mid\{\eta_i^t\}]$ → the conclusion also asserts integrability of the integrand; reason: the source takes the expectation as defined; in Lean a non-integrable integrand has integral $0$
--   - encoding: $u_i$ continuously differentiable in $x_i$ with $v_i=\nabla_{x_i}u_i$ (Definition 3.1) → $v_i(x)$ is a gradient of $x_i\mapsto u_i(x_i;x_{-i})$ within $\mathcal X_i$ (`HasGradientWithinAt`), $v$ continuous on $\mathcal X$; each $\mathcal X_i$ nonempty; reason: costs are defined on $\mathcal X$ only, so the derivative is taken within $\mathcal X_i$; nonempty action sets are the standing convention (Algorithm 3 starts from $x_i^1\in\mathcal X_i$)
--   - encoding: agents $\mathcal N=\{1,\dots,N\}$, $x\in\prod_i\mathbb R^{d_i}$ with the Euclidean norm, $\beta$-strongly monotone, rounds $t\ge1$ → agents `Fin N` with $N\ge1$, joint space `PiLp 2` ($\|x\|^2=\sum_i\|x_i\|^2$), $\beta>0$, $T\ge1$; $\max_{1\le i\le N}$ is the real supremum over `Fin N`; reason: Lean conventions; the same instances
-- source:
--   Jordan, Lin & Zhou, Adaptive, Doubly Optimal No-Regret Learning in Strongly Monotone and Exp-Concave Games with Gradient Feedback, arXiv:2310.14085v4, Lemma 3.10, p.16; proof in Appendix D, pp.40–41

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_AdaOGD_IsMAAdaOGDRun

namespace AdaOGD

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

/-- Lemma 3.10 (Jordan, Lin & Zhou, arXiv:2310.14085v4, p.16; proof in Appendix D, pp.40–41):
suppose that a continuous game is `β`-strongly monotone, the feedback satisfies
`𝔼[ξᵗ | xᵗ] = v(xᵗ)`, `𝔼[‖ξᵗ‖² | xᵗ] ≤ G²` and `‖x − x'‖ ≤ D` on `𝒳`, and the iterates
`{xᵗ}` are generated by Algorithm 3. Then, with `x⋆` the Nash equilibrium, almost surely
`∑ᵢ ηᵀᵢ 𝔼[‖xᵀᵢ − x⋆ᵢ‖² | {ηᵗᵢ}_{1≤i≤N,1≤t≤T}] ≤ ∑ᵢ η¹ᵢ‖x¹ᵢ − x⋆ᵢ‖²
  + D² ∑_{t=1}^{T−1} max{0, max_{1≤i≤N}{ηᵗ⁺¹ᵢ − ηᵗᵢ} − 2β} + G² ∑_{t=1}^{T−1} max_{1≤i≤N}{1/ηᵗ⁺¹ᵢ}`.

**Formalization Note** The conditioning σ-algebra is `etaSigma M T`, generated by the step sizes
`ηᵗᵢ`, `1 ≤ i ≤ N`, `1 ≤ t ≤ T`, as printed. The feedback model is `IsOracle` (Reading 1:
conditioning on `xᵗ` and all step sizes, as in Appendix D, p.40); `𝒳ᵢ` are the bounded sets of
Definition 3.1, the run and `x⋆` being hypotheses; `η¹ᵢ = 1`. The
statement is for every Nash equilibrium `x⋆` (there is exactly one, `nash_exists_unique`). The
conclusion also asserts that each `‖xᵀᵢ − x⋆ᵢ‖²` is integrable, so that the conditional
expectations are those of integrable functions. `max_{1≤i≤N}` is the real supremum over `Fin N`. -/
theorem lemma_3_10 {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) {d : Fin N → ℕ} (G : ContinuousGame d)
    {β Gc D : ℝ} (hβ : 0 < β) (hGc : 0 < Gc) (hD : 0 < D)
    (hmono : IsStronglyMonotone G β) (hdiam : DiamLe G D)
    {Ω : Type*} {m0 : MeasurableSpace Ω} {P : Measure Ω} [IsProbabilityMeasure P]
    {T : ℕ} (hT : 1 ≤ T)
    {M : Fin N → ℕ → Ω → ℕ} (hM : IsGeomDraws P T M)
    {x1 : Joint d} {ξ x : ℕ → Ω → Joint d} (hξ : IsOracle G P T Gc M x ξ)
    (hx : IsMAAdaOGDRun G T M x1 ξ x)
    (xs : Joint d) (hxs : IsNash G xs) :
    (∀ i : Fin N, Integrable (fun ω => ‖x T ω i - xs i‖ ^ 2) P) ∧
    (fun ω => ∑ i : Fin N,
        eta M i T ω * condExp (etaSigma M T) P (fun ω' => ‖x T ω' i - xs i‖ ^ 2) ω)
      ≤ᵐ[P] fun ω =>
        ∑ i : Fin N, eta M i 1 ω * ‖x 1 ω i - xs i‖ ^ 2
          + D ^ 2 * ∑ t ∈ Finset.Icc 1 (T - 1),
              max 0 ((⨆ i : Fin N, (eta M i (t + 1) ω - eta M i t ω)) - 2 * β)
          + Gc ^ 2 * ∑ t ∈ Finset.Icc 1 (T - 1), (⨆ i : Fin N, 1 / eta M i (t + 1) ω) := by sorry

end AdaOGD
